<!--
    Type campaign, item `boolbyte`: a byte whose every use is a truth test is
    declared `char`.  coreutils basename -O0 shows it on a scored parameter:

        void sub_2b45(unsigned long a0, long a1, char a2)   // DWARF: _Bool use_nuls
        {
          ...
          v1 = (a2) ? 0 : 10;
        }

    Root cause (P5, type propagation).  `TYPE_BOOL` is only ever minted as an
    op's OUTPUT type - every `booloutput` op's `getOutputLocal` is
    `getBase(size, TYPE_BOOL)` - and nothing mints it for the value being
    compared: `TypeOpEqual::getInputLocal` votes `getBase(1, TYPE_INT)` for that,
    and the cached one-byte `TYPE_INT` is the ASCII `char`, because
    `TypeFactory::cacheCoreTypes` prefers a character type over `int1`.  So the
    `getLocalType` fold has no bool candidate at all, and the byte comes out
    `char` (or `uint1`, when an unsigned vote wins the same fold).

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation".  The P0 assertion
    `option boolbyte on|off` decides whether that candidate is offered.

    The fixture is the same shape on SysV x86-64, plus three controls:

        flagparam:  test dil,dil; je ...
                    the witness - the byte is only ever branched on
        bytearith:  test dil,dil; je ...; movzx eax,dil; add eax,3
                    a control - the same byte is also widened and added, which
                    no truth value is, so the rule must decline
        lowbit:     test dil,1; je ...
                    a control - the byte is tested for its LOW BIT, which is a
                    parity test on an unproven byte rather than a truth test, so
                    the rule must decline (the INT_AND against 1 counts only for
                    a value already proven to be 0 or 1)
        flagstore:  test dil,dil; je ...; mov [rsi],dil
                    a control - the byte is branched on AND stored through a
                    pointer, and a store is not a truth use, so the rule must
                    decline
        callfill:   mov byte [rsp+0xf],0; lea rdi,[rsp+0xf]; call touch;
                    movzx eax,[rsp+0xf]; test al,al
                    a control - the stack byte is provably 0 before the call and
                    truth-tested after it, and `touch` writes 200 through the
                    pointer it is handed.  The INDIRECT the call leaves on that
                    storage is NOT value-preserving identity, so neither half of
                    the walk may cross it and the rule must decline
        callslot:   mov [rsp+0xf],dil; lea rdi,[rsp+0xf]; call touch;
                    movzx eax,[rsp+0xf]; test al,al
                    the same control from the parameter side - a byte parameter
                    copied into a slot the callee is handed the address of.
                    Crossing the INDIRECT here declares the PARAMETER bool, and
                    a caller passing 200 then sees 1
        touch:      mov byte [rdi],200; ret
                    the callee the two controls hand a pointer to
        truncjoin:  setg [rsp+7] on one path, mov [rsp+7],al (al = x>>31) on the
                    other; cmp [rsp+7],0
                    the RENDERING case - a stack byte a comparison already makes
                    `bool`, whose other def truncates a four-byte value into it.
                    `CastStrategyC::isSubpieceCast` has no TYPE_BOOL destination
                    in its list, so the printer falls to the functional arm and
                    emits the raw `SUB41(x,0)` p-code intrinsic - an undeclared
                    identifier, not compilable C.  The option supplies the missing
                    arm and the truncation prints as the `(bool)` cast it is

    Pass 1 (`option boolbyte off`) reproduces the bug; pass 2 (`on`) is the fix.
    All five controls are identical in both passes.
-->
